WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:07.129
Gerald Ford: In my judgment from my practical experience
in the Congress of 25 years and my two-and-a-half

00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:17.660
years in the White House, I firmly believe
that trying to put on paper a precise procedure

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:26.749
by which things have to be done in a crisis
is impractical in the first place.

00:00:26.749 --> 00:00:35.700
And secondly, I believe it's unconstitutional
as an undercutting of a president's responsibility

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.480
as commander-in-chief of our military forces.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:47.140
And thirdly, I would add, and this is probably
the most important point.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:56.150
A President of the United States under any
and all circumstances has to maximize his

00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:04.350
efforts either to maintain the peace on the
one hand or to achieve peace on the other.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:14.100
I firmly believe as a practical matter that
the War Powers Resolution with all its requirements

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.920
handicaps a president.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:28.479
Announcer: From the nation's capital, the
American Enterprise Institute for Public Policy

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:34.030
Research presents Public Policy Forums, a
series of programs featuring the nation's

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:38.460
top authorities presenting their differing
views on the vital issues which confronts

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.460
us.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.659
Today's topic, War Powers and the Constitution.

00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:49.969
John Charles Daly: This public policy forum part of a series presented by the American Enterprise Institute

00:01:49.969 --> 00:01:55.040
is concerned with the war powers of the Congress
and the president.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:01.159
The issue is one our founding fathers preferred
after much debate not to spell out precisely

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:07.299
in the Constitution but which the Congress
in the last three decades has been determined

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:13.680
to interpret and define, our subject, War
Powers and the Constitution.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.629
In broad strokes, the Constitution stipulates
that the Congress shall have the power to

00:02:18.629 --> 00:02:24.000
declare war to raise and support armies and
to provide and maintain a navy.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.239
The president says the Constitution shall
be commander-in-chief of the armed forces.

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:34.959
Now, interestingly, the Constitutional Convention
hammering out the language on War Powers discussed

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:38.769
granting Congress the power to make war.

00:02:38.769 --> 00:02:43.209
But ultimately, it decided on to declare war.

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:50.940
Again, in broad strokes, the War Powers Act
of 1973 requires the president, one, to consult

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:58.060
with the Congress "in every possible instance"
before US armed forces enter hostilities,

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:04.780
two, to report to the Congress within 48 hours
when forces are so committed, and three, to

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:11.120
terminate the engagement within 60 days if
Congress hasn't declared war or specifically

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.659
authorized use of the armed forces.

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:20.730
Finally, it allows Congress within those 60
days to direct the President to withdraw by

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:27.609
resolution passed by both houses which will
not require presidential signature where improved

00:03:27.609 --> 00:03:32.549
lies the security and best interest of the
United States in the allocation of War Powers.

00:03:32.549 --> 00:03:40.290
But to chart a course out of this dilemma,
we have a distinguished panel, to my far right,

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.769
Representative Lee H Hamilton, Democrat of
Indiana, member of the House Foreign Affairs

00:03:44.769 --> 00:03:45.769
Committee.

00:03:45.769 --> 00:03:51.731
He also serves on the Permanent Select Committee
on Intelligence, to my immediate right, Senator

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:57.390
Charles Mathias Jr., Republican and senior
senator of Maryland.

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:02.090
Senator Mathias is a member of the Senate
Foreign Relations Committee and second ranking

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:07.590
member of the Judiciary Committee, to my immediate
left Representative Dick Cheney, a Republican

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.060
of Wyoming.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:14.040
Mr. Cheney served in the Ford administration
as White House Chief of Staff.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.750
Congressman Cheney was elected to serve as
chairman of the House Republican Policy Committee,

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:23.290
and to my far left Lieutenant General Brent
Scowcroft.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:27.990
Before retiring from military service in the
Air Force, General Scowcroft served in the

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:31.620
White House as President Ford's national security
advisor.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:36.110
More recently, General Scowcroft served as
chairman of President Reagan's Commission

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:37.840
on strategic forces.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:45.130
He was also coordinator of AEI's 1981 project
on American vital interests in regions of

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:46.130
conflict.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:51.360
Now to begin, let us hear first the views
of our 38th president.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:58.110
Gerald Ford: Let me take a particular
or a specific example of what happened under

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:03.110
the War Powers Resolution during my administration.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:16.370
I'm sure people recall the seizure of the
merchant vessel of Mayagüez in 1975 when

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:23.270
the Cambodians illegally under international
law seized an American merchant vessel off

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:26.860
the shores of Cambodia.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:33.660
That precipitated, of course, a challenge
to my administration.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.240
How could we get the crew back without any
loss of life?

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:42.330
How we could recover the ship itself.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:50.800
Under the War Powers Resolution, we would
have been hamstrung and handicapped in trying

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:54.520
to go through all of the procedures which
were required.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:58.360
We had to take action, and we did.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:04.900
And the net result is the crew and the ship
were recovered from the enemy.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:10.690
Now, that doesn't mean we didn't sit down
and consult with the Congress.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:19.099
We tried to find them, and we were able to
have an opportunity to have them come to the

00:06:19.099 --> 00:06:20.360
Cabinet Room.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:23.300
And we did tell them what the facts were.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:28.270
We outlined what we were going to do, which
was successful.

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:32.370
Members of Congress have many, many other
duties.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:40.389
Their principal responsibility is not to be
commander-in-chief, therefore, their information

00:06:40.389 --> 00:06:46.410
as to what's transpiring on a minute-by-minute
or an hour-by-hour basis is not available

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:47.660
to them.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:55.120
A president on the other hand, where the Constitution
gives him the responsibility of being commander-in-chief,

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:01.990
he has to know by the hour at least what's
happening so he can act responsibly.

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:06.440
You can't have 535 commander and chiefs.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:11.229
You can't have 535 secretaries of state.

00:07:11.229 --> 00:07:18.949
Their duties under our Constitution are different,
different, important but different from the

00:07:18.949 --> 00:07:19.949
president.

00:07:19.949 --> 00:07:26.630
John Charles Daly: And now panel, I would post the same
question to each of you in turn.

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:32.610
Has the world changed so radically in 200
years that the Congress should attempt what

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:37.979
our founding fathers were not willing to do
in writing the Constitution, to specifically

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:43.699
define and catalog the prerogatives and powers
of the Congress and the executive in the exercise

00:07:43.699 --> 00:07:44.699
of War Powers?

00:07:44.699 --> 00:07:46.080
Will you start Senator Mathias?

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:52.639
Charles Mathias, Jr.: All right, would take the
issue I think with the question as it's framed.

00:07:52.639 --> 00:07:59.220
The authors of the Constitution made it very
clear where the power over war and peace would

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:00.970
rest.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:08.229
It's true that they made the president commander-in-chief
of the armed forces, but they vested the decision

00:08:08.229 --> 00:08:12.860
as to war peace solely in the Congress of
the United States.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:17.789
They gave to the Congress the power to make
rules and regulations for the governance of

00:08:17.789 --> 00:08:20.159
the Armed Forces.

00:08:20.159 --> 00:08:28.389
They provided that the Congress should make
rules for captures on land or water.

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:34.610
They provided that the Congress should issue
letters of marque, in other words authorizing

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.940
a warlike acts by private vessels.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:43.169
So there is a panoply of power vested in the
Congress by the constitution, which makes

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:51.040
it clear that the founders wanted the Congress
to control the issues of war and peace.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:57.890
And what the War Powers Act does is merely
to implement in a modern framework what I

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:01.589
feel to be the very clear constitutional purpose.

00:09:01.589 --> 00:09:03.779
John Charles Daly: Congressman Cheney.

00:09:03.779 --> 00:09:09.940
Dick Cheney: I would take strong exception,
John, with the views of my friend and colleague

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.000
Senator Mathias.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:16.970
I really believe that the notion of a declaration
of war is almost an outmoded concept under

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:21.810
virtually any set of circumstances we can
conceive of under which the president would

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:24.310
decide to commit troops to combat.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:27.820
I think in many respects, it's a legacy of
the Vietnam War.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:32.860
It's a statute that was adopted in an effort
to keep us out of the next war based upon

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:35.930
a misperception of how we got into the last
one.

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:41.079
It was put on the books I think with the expectation
that had it been on the books in 1964 and

00:09:41.079 --> 00:09:43.940
'65, this nation would never have been involved
in Southeast Asia.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.510
I simply don't believe that's not the case.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:49.940
Congress consistently and continually supported
that adventure.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.670
And the War Powers Act hasn't changed that
at all.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:10:00.410
I do believe very firmly that the War Powers
Act is an unwise and virtually an unworkable

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:04.370
intrusion by the legislative branch into the
powers and prerogatives that a president needs

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:07.360
to lead the United States in a very dangerous
and hostile world.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:08.560
John Charles Daly: Congressman Hamilton.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.810
Lee Hamilton: John, I support the
War Powers Act.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:17.839
Edwin Corwin, the great constitutional scholar
said that the Constitution is an invitation

00:10:17.839 --> 00:10:23.290
to the Congress and the President to struggle
for the privilege of conducting American foreign

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:24.730
policy.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:30.370
And I think the War Powers Act simply reflects
that struggle.

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:38.060
The purpose of the War Powers Act is to guarantee
that the Congress and the president will exercise

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:44.110
their collective judgment on the most important
question that government faces, and that is

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:47.009
whether or not you go to war.

00:10:47.009 --> 00:10:55.180
In this day and age with the extreme complexity
of the modern world, the very rapid growth

00:10:55.180 --> 00:11:04.860
of America's responsibilities in that world
and the ambiguous nature of so many international

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:11.910
conflicts, it seems to me that kind of a world
demands that the Congress and the president

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:18.490
should exercise their joint judgment, their
collective judgment on the critical question

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:20.720
of whether or not you go to war.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:26.050
It's not a decision that ought to be made
by one man or woman even if that man or woman

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:27.920
is the president of the United States.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.980
It ought to be a collective judgment.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:31.530
John Charles Daly: General Scowcroft.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:37.870
Brent Scowcroft: I think it's not so much
that the world has changed in 200 years.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:43.060
But the United States and its role in the
world has changed substantially.

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:49.830
The Constitution did not legislate a government
designed for maximum efficiency.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:56.100
It legislated a government designed to protect
the rights of the individual against an overweening

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.970
government, and it does that very well.

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:08.139
The problem is that the inefficiencies that
that kind of freedom protecting innovation

00:12:08.139 --> 00:12:15.519
dictates makes it very difficult for a world
power involved in many areas everywhere around

00:12:15.519 --> 00:12:20.270
the world to discharge its responsibilities.

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:28.230
The struggle, as congressman Hamilton indicated,
between the executive and the legislature

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:32.380
has swung back and forth over our history
of predominance one way or another.

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:40.899
I think that in very practical terms, the
War Powers Act is the reflection of a time

00:12:40.899 --> 00:12:47.500
when following a series of dynamic and activist
presidents, the presidency was beset both

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:53.149
by Vietnam, by Watergate, and the Congress
in effect seized an opportunity to make an

00:12:53.149 --> 00:13:00.639
inroad and to change the balance that had
prevailed essentially since Roosevelt.

00:13:00.639 --> 00:13:08.160
I do believe that we must find ways that the
executive and the legislature can cooperate

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:13.880
in a discharge of the awesome business of
conflict.

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:20.040
But I think the War Powers Act in the way
it does it, in fact, not only ties the hand

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:23.540
of the president but in a sense makes conflict
more likely.

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:24.540
John Charles Daly: Senator Mathias.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:30.839
Charles Mathias, Jr.: If I may say that I agree
with Dick Cheney that the War Powers Act grew

00:13:30.839 --> 00:13:32.800
out of the experience of Vietnam.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:36.010
I lived through that as a member of Congress.

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:40.030
I voted for the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution.

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:46.380
And I think it's fair to say that most of
us who did vote for it felt in some way find

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:51.360
me, as the facts were revealed, that we had
been bamboozled and that there was no mechanism,

00:13:51.360 --> 00:14:00.459
no process by which the Congress could come
to grips with a situation of that sort once

00:14:00.459 --> 00:14:04.520
the Act has been done, once the resolution
has been passed.

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:13.269
I remember once riding in an automobile with
President Johnson and expressing some doubts

00:14:13.269 --> 00:14:14.269
about the war.

00:14:14.269 --> 00:14:16.380
And he said, "Here, you voted for this resolution.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:21.850
Now, if you've got the guts to step up and
introduce a resolution to repeal it."

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:29.220
And he reached in his pocket, and he pulled
out a copy at one handy.

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:33.839
We were really without a handle in which to
debate these issues.

00:14:33.839 --> 00:14:38.889
And once troops are committed and casualties
are being sustained in a level of patriotism

00:14:38.889 --> 00:14:41.990
and the country is up, it's very hard to grab
hold of.

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:48.509
So what the War Powers Act provides is a mechanism
and a process by which the Congress can periodically

00:14:48.509 --> 00:14:52.829
review our involvement in foreign military
adventures.

00:14:52.829 --> 00:14:58.519
And I think this is entirely consistent with
what the founders of the Constitution proposed.

00:14:58.519 --> 00:15:04.889
James Madison who probably knew more about
the Constitution than any man who ever lived

00:15:04.889 --> 00:15:12.420
once wrote to Thomas Jefferson that the Constitution
supposes what the history of all governments

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:20.220
demonstrates, that the executive is the branch
of power most interested in war and most prone

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:22.410
to it.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:28.149
It has accorded me with studied care vested
the question of war in the legislature.

00:15:28.149 --> 00:15:29.959
John Charles Daly: Congressman Cheney.

00:15:29.959 --> 00:15:36.029
Dick Cheney: I guess I have to take
exception with Mac.

00:15:36.029 --> 00:15:38.269
I think the War Powers action...

00:15:38.269 --> 00:15:41.300
Charles Mathias, Jr.: Take exception with me but
you have trouble taking exception with Madison.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:45.579
Dick Cheney: Well, obviously, there
are differences in terms of our interpretation

00:15:45.579 --> 00:15:49.360
of what the Constitution provides and with
respect to whether the president of the legislative

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:52.050
body has prime authority in this area.

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:56.260
And I think most members of Congress, even
individuals like myself who have serious problems

00:15:56.260 --> 00:16:02.490
with the War Powers Act, believe that the
language of the Act that talks about consultation

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:04.639
and notification is indeed perfectly appropriate.

00:16:04.639 --> 00:16:08.589
It's the notion of Congress being able to
move in in a very short period of time and

00:16:08.589 --> 00:16:12.769
terminate that commitment that bothers me
a great deal.

00:16:12.769 --> 00:16:19.430
I look upon the Congress as being all too
often swayed by public opinion of the moment,

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:26.759
that there is not a certainty there are or
a conviction, the kind of determination or

00:16:26.759 --> 00:16:29.500
decision-making that would let them be an
equal partner with the president with all

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:33.630
of the resources that he has available to
him in terms of making a broad policy decision

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:34.860
that'll hold for a year or two.

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:40.100
For example, of the Grenada situation, where
the Speaker of the House at the outset called

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:45.440
it gunboat diplomacy and within a matter of
two weeks had changed his mind and said that,

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:48.829
indeed, President Reagan had done exactly
the right thing in sending troops into Grenada.

00:16:48.829 --> 00:16:50.160
Charles Mathias, Jr.: He was right in the first
time.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:55.459
Dick Cheney: I think that Congress,
unfortunately, is a pretty weak read to lead

00:16:55.459 --> 00:16:57.730
on in terms of that kind of decision-making.

00:16:57.730 --> 00:17:03.690
I also would argue, Mac, that if the War Powers
Act had been on the books in 1964 when the

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:08.030
Tonkin Gulf Resolution was adopted, that it
would not have altered the outcome at all,

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:12.270
that Congress did indeed have the opportunity
year in and year out throughout the history

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:17.400
of our involvement in Southeast Asia to reject
the course we were upon and consistently year

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:20.920
after year after year appropriated the funds
for that exercise.

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:25.310
So I really think it's a bit of a misreading
of history to suggest that the War Powers

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.150
Act might have altered the outcome in Southeast
Asia.

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:28.150
John Charles Daly: Congressman Hamilton.

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:31.450
Lee Hamilton: I think the real question
is whether the war-making power ought to be

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:36.740
a decision made by one person or made by the
president and the Congress.

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:38.160
That seems to me to be the heart of it.

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:43.790
And the War Powers Act simply says that the
Congress is gonna participate in that decision.

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:44.860
Why shouldn't it?

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:49.350
It seems to me that the guiding principle
is that a democracy should go to war only

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:53.300
with the consent of the people, only with
the consent of the people as expressed by

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:56.440
their elected representatives.

00:17:56.440 --> 00:18:02.050
So I think the War Powers Act is totally consistent
with the Constitution and with the founding

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:03.050
fathers.

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:07.510
Now, General Scowcroft speaks a moment ago
about tying the hand of the president.

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:15.090
Well, quite frankly, that's one of the things
we want to do, tying the hands of the president.

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:23.070
We want to make the president say, to stop,
to think, to listen before he commits us to

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:24.070
war.

00:18:24.070 --> 00:18:25.070
That's what we want to do.

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:28.200
John Charles Daly: Is there time recently when a president
did not consult before committing troops?

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.940
Dick Cheney: Wow, look what happened
in Grenada.

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:35.800
The president called in the speaker and the
majority leader at 8:00 in the evening.

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:37.610
He did not consult with him.

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:39.150
The Speaker of the House has said that.

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:44.780
He said they were informed that the Marines
were landing at 5:30 the next morning.

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:46.780
There wasn't any consultation there.

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:49.020
It was just a matter of informing.

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:50.840
Informing is not consultation.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:54.330
At 8:00, the Speaker learns about of it.

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:58.940
The rest of us learned about it in the newspaper,
and the Marines go in at 5:30.

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:00.110
That's not consultation.

00:19:00.110 --> 00:19:05.340
When Jimmy Carter, President Carter, sent
the troops into Iran for a rescue mission,

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:06.430
he didn't consult with the Congress.

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:10.080
Brent Scowcroft: The Congress already has
adequate resource.

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:11.820
It does control the purse strings.

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:13.740
It does raise and maintain armies.

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:21.470
If it doesn't like what the executive is doing,
it has all the power it needs if it can get

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:24.520
itself together to do it, to work as well.

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:26.570
Dick Cheney: That's a very slow power
though.

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:28.510
It takes us a long time to that.

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:33.710
The advantage of the War Powers Act is within
the 60-day period or within the 90-day period,

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:36.170
the Congress has to act to approve or disapprove.

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:37.880
Brent Scowcroft: No, it doesn't have to
act.

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:39.400
That is one of the key problems.

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:44.510
If it doesn't act, by its own inaction, it
makes a decision.

00:19:44.510 --> 00:19:50.270
Now, that it seems to me is a supine way for
the Congress to work as well.

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:58.660
But the real practical effect of the War Powers
Act is, in the real world, the president sometimes

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:04.090
has to introduce forces into an area to try
to prevent a conflict, to try to stabilize

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:07.920
a region, to try to prevent something from
happening.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:14.580
In terms of demonstrating our resolve, by
telling our potential opponents that all they

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:21.580
have to do is wait 60 days and see what happens,
you have undercut his ability to demonstrate

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:25.240
that kind of resolve that could prevent a
conflict from occurring.

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:27.000
Dick Cheney: No, I disagree with that,
General.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:31.170
What you've done is strengthen the decision
to commit the troops.

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:35.920
If a president commits troops and he does
not have the support of the Congress and he

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:39.750
does not have the support of the American
people, he's not gonna be able to sustain

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:43.250
that commitment and he's not gonna be able
to keep those troops there.

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:46.120
We've learned that lesson very, very hard.

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:50.970
If you're going to go into war, then you would
better be very sure that you've got the support

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:52.460
of the American people.

00:20:52.460 --> 00:20:56.270
If you don't have the support of the American
people, you're not gonna be able to sustain

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:57.270
it.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:59.350
How do you get the support of the American
people?

00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:01.700
One of the ways is to have the support of
the Congress.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:07.380
John Charles Daly: Let me if I may ask, ask that we focus
in on Lebanon.

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:11.980
You played a major part in a compromise that
was worked out the terms of which were never

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:16.460
specific as I understand it on whether the
War Powers Act was being implemented or not

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:17.460
implemented.

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:23.710
But it has another peculiar framework to consider,
which is that we were not introducing troops

00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:25.330
into Lebanon into hostilities.

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:28.760
We were a peacekeeping force which we joined
together with other nations.

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:34.530
Charles Mathias, Jr.: I think the War Powers Act makes a very
positive contribution to the management of

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:38.200
the Lebanese situation.

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:44.750
Far from perfect, as you point out, there's
some ambiguities both in the way the situation

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:47.830
came about and how we structured the response.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:53.540
But for example, we made it very clear that
the troops on the beach were to be limited

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:57.620
to those who were there who were present on
the day the resolution passed.

00:21:57.620 --> 00:21:58.840
John Charles Daly: In terms of numbers.

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:00.900
Charles Mathias, Jr.: In terms of numbers.

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:05.590
There are ambiguities about offshore forces
about the naval forces.

00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:10.290
But the troops on the beach, there was absolutely
no question about.

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:11.900
That was totally specific.

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:17.510
If we go beyond the number of Marines who
were originally there, there has to be real

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:20.060
congressional consultation more than just
advice.

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:21.060
Lee Hamilton: John.

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:22.060
John Charles Daly: Yes, Mr. Congressman.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:26.330
Lee Hamilton: John, if I understood you correctly,
you said that the War Powers Act was not implemented

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:27.900
in the Lebanon situation.

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:28.900
I think it was.

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:33.280
John Charles Daly: No, what I said was that there seems
to be some confusion about whether it was

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:36.420
applied or not or as accepted as applied by
both part.

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:41.590
Lee Hamilton: Now, it's very clear in the resolution
that was passed that section 4A1 was implemented

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:44.160
in the resolution that we passed.

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:47.120
President Reagan when he signed it, did so
with the reservations.

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:50.870
He obviously had some questions about it,
but he did sign it.

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:56.270
And this represents the first full use of
the War Powers Act.

00:22:56.270 --> 00:23:02.320
As I understand it, that triggered the 60-day
clause, that triggered the 90-day period.

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:08.750
And now we have the first president on record
as having implemented the War Powers Act.

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:10.140
Brent Scowcroft: He did not accept the war policy.

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:15.320
Lee Hamilton: No, the president
when...he accepted it by signing the bill.

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:19.930
Now, when he signed the bill, he made a statement
about his reservations about it.

00:23:19.930 --> 00:23:24.100
But the fact is he signed the bill, and it
is the law of the land.

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:27.060
And the law specifically triggers the War
Powers Act.

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:31.720
Brent Scowcroft: Mr. Hamilton, that's like
the veto, single house veto.

00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:36.750
The president has signed numbers and numbers
of bills, many presidents have with that in

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:41.220
it, many times stating that there is a constitutional
problem.

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:43.640
That doesn't mean that he acquiesces
in that.

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:44.640
John Charles Daly: Congressman Cheney.

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:48.760
Dick Cheney: The fact, John, that two
such obviously intelligent and bright and

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:52.250
distinguished gentlemen as General Scowcroft
and Congressman Hamilton can't even agree

00:23:52.250 --> 00:23:57.270
on what we did in the case of the Lebanon
Resolution strikes me as pretty hard evidence

00:23:57.270 --> 00:24:01.630
that it is a very difficult piece of legislation
and probably unworkable.

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:06.420
I think from this...and I would certainly
support Brent's views in terms of what the

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:07.760
administration did.

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:13.100
Also, I was fascinated by the debate on the
issue when it came to the floor of the house

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:17.620
because the debate, if I recall, had nothing
to do with should we be there or shouldn't

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:18.620
we be there.

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:21.540
Nobody offered an alternative that said everybody
out now.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:27.520
The alternative was it's gonna be 60 days
or 90 days or 180 days or 18 months.

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:33.180
And oftentimes, the debate over how many days
it should be and whether we ought to add a

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:36.960
month or take a month off has very little
to do with the substance of policy.

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:40.930
It has a lot to do with a posture to my colleagues
and whether or not you can build a majority

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:43.650
for six months instead of nine months.

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:45.540
I think it's a bad way to make policy.

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:50.510
I also think, as Mac pointed out, the limitation
on troops on the beach is very unwise.

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:54.890
If you're gonna commit military force to a
potentially hostile situation, one of the

00:24:54.890 --> 00:24:58.950
key ingredients to having it have any effect
at all is the uncertainty that it creates

00:24:58.950 --> 00:24:59.960
on the minds of your adversary.

00:24:59.960 --> 00:25:03.510
And if you have at the outset said, "Yes,
we're gonna commit troops, but you can't have

00:25:03.510 --> 00:25:08.540
any more than 1,600 on the beach under any
foreseeable set of circumstances," it seems

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:11.770
to me you've defeated the purpose of having
any troops there in the first place.

00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:12.770
John Charles Daly: Senator.

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:23.280
Charles Mathias, Jr.: Two points, one this time
factor, of course, is not an absolute termination

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:24.370
of the operation.

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:30.000
The time factor is a period of time after
which the Congress will make a review and

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.580
after which there will be further consultation.

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:37.830
And as Lee Hamilton says, a joint decision
by the Congress and the executive as to whether

00:25:37.830 --> 00:25:42.850
or not the operation shall be continued and
maintained or whether it should be terminated.

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:48.590
Now, it is the point that never came in Vietnam
in a formal structured way.

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:51.370
And that's the thing that was missing in the
Vietnam debate.

00:25:51.370 --> 00:25:54.040
The executive could simply roll over the Congress.

00:25:54.040 --> 00:26:00.120
The other point that I wanted to make was
this, that of course, it's true as Dick says,

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:08.290
that if you say we're gonna get out in a limited
period of time, that that may make your adversary

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:11.640
procrastinate and not come to terms with reality.

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:19.500
But it also in the Lebanese situation could
make your friends there rest on their arms,

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:25.670
not really engage in the hard political process
of bringing about a reconciliation of the

00:26:25.670 --> 00:26:30.510
conflicting interests within Lebanon, because
the longer they keep us there, the longer

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:35.100
they don't have to come to terms with the
realities of political life in Lebanon.

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:41.200
So I think that there are two sides to the
question of the uncertainties that are created

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:42.540
by a limited time frame.

00:26:42.540 --> 00:26:48.350
John Charles Daly: What about the big...at least in my
view, the big constitutional question, Mac,

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:53.400
which is the right reserved to the Congress
to direct the president to pull out even in

00:26:53.400 --> 00:27:00.860
the 60 days and constitute in effect a legislative
veto of his actions with the Supreme Court

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:07.760
holding certainly in other circumstances that
the legislative veto was unconstitutional.

00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:08.960
Does that bother you?

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:12.570
Dick Cheney: I would have to say that
I think that the decision that the court did

00:27:12.570 --> 00:27:19.980
hand down does, in fact, not bode well for
the legislative veto features of the War Powers

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:20.980
Act.

00:27:20.980 --> 00:27:24.930
And that the War Powers Act in terms of the
provision you cite would require the President

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:29.610
to take an action by a simple majority vote
of both houses of Congress and that that is

00:27:29.610 --> 00:27:32.520
indeed a violation of the separation of powers.

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:36.670
Lee Hamilton: I basically agree with Dick's observation
on it.

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:42.300
The provision of the War Powers Act which
permits the Congress to pull out the troops

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:47.850
with a concurrent resolution is highly suspect
now with the Supreme Court decision.

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:51.570
And my guess would be that it's probably unconstitutional.

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:55.030
There is a separability clause in the War
Powers Act.

00:27:55.030 --> 00:28:00.870
The likelihood I think would be that the balance
of the Act might still stand although that

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:03.050
may be in dispute as well.

00:28:03.050 --> 00:28:07.630
But I think Dick is quite right in saying
that provision may very well be unconstitutional.

00:28:07.630 --> 00:28:12.980
Charles Mathias, Jr.: Of course, that doesn't affect a large
part of the of the War Powers Act.

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:19.670
The original statutory 60-day limitation on
the use of troops overseas is not affected

00:28:19.670 --> 00:28:22.380
by the Supreme Court decision.

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:25.480
That's pristine and untouched.

00:28:25.480 --> 00:28:31.120
The provisions on the growth of forces or
the change of mission or the change of scope

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:34.490
of the mission, all of that is untouched by
the Supreme Court decision.

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:38.270
Brent Scowcroft: I don't have any particular comment on
the chartered decision, but it seems to me

00:28:38.270 --> 00:28:44.651
that's only one of several areas where the
constitutionality of the Act is, well, at

00:28:44.651 --> 00:28:45.651
best dubious.

00:28:45.651 --> 00:28:52.400
John Charles Daly: Mr. Congressman or Senator, would you look kindly upon members of the Congress specifically

00:28:52.400 --> 00:28:55.740
serving with the National Security Council
and feel that that would give you...?

00:28:55.740 --> 00:29:00.590
Lee Hamilton: No, I think that causes real constitutional
problems.

00:29:00.590 --> 00:29:04.880
We are committed to the philosophy of separation
of powers.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:08.050
And I don't see how you could have that kind
of an overlap.

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:10.660
And I think that would cause problems.

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:16.710
Consultation, I think...I don't find as much
trouble with it as you seem to.

00:29:16.710 --> 00:29:25.870
I think it was entirely proper to call in
the legislative leadership and if the impropriety

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:30.270
occurred not in the people that were chosen
in my view but the fact that they didn't consult.

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:33.490
Dick Cheney: John, if it's appropriate can we pursue
just for a moment the consultation?

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:34.490
John Charles Daly: But General Scowcroft wanted...

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:35.490
Brent Scowcroft: No, that's all right.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:36.490
John Charles Daly: You wanna go on?

00:29:36.490 --> 00:29:37.490
Brent Scowcroft: Go ahead.

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:38.490
You gonna talk about consultation.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:40.060
Dick Cheney: Yes, I wanted to follow on with what
Mac said.

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:44.760
And the problem part, Mac, is trying to define
what would have been acceptable.

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:46.840
Let's take Grenada for an example.

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:50.510
You had a situation in which the president
believed American lives were at stake.

00:29:50.510 --> 00:29:55.070
How under those circumstances when the whole
train of events only lasted a few days from

00:29:55.070 --> 00:30:00.610
the time the problem arose until action was
taken what would be an acceptable consultation

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:01.610
process?

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:03.630
Charles Mathias, Jr.: I'll tell you exactly.

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:08.910
The critical decisions were being made down
there on the Golf Course in Atlanta.

00:30:08.910 --> 00:30:15.020
And the speaker plays golf.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:20.430
I mean, if you wanted to really have a consultation,
you'll, "Come down here and play around the

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:22.280
golf with me."

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:26.290
And he could join in those conversations that
took place in the clubhouse and on the ninth

00:30:26.290 --> 00:30:27.290
hole.

00:30:27.290 --> 00:30:31.290
Dick Cheney: Even with the consultation that the speaker
had, and I was present the morning of the

00:30:31.290 --> 00:30:36.110
event when the president met with a larger
group and he had bipartisan leadership...

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:37.110
Charles Mathias, Jr.: After the fact.

00:30:37.110 --> 00:30:41.590
Dick Cheney: This was at the time that the invasion
was actually underway already.

00:30:41.590 --> 00:30:45.410
There wasn't one single member in the room
of either party, some 30 members of the House

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:49.290
and Senate in the room, not one single member
in the room who raised a question about the

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:50.290
operation.

00:30:50.290 --> 00:30:54.050
But three days later, the speaker was out
publicly condemning the president for gunboat

00:30:54.050 --> 00:30:55.050
diplomacy.

00:30:55.050 --> 00:31:00.870
And 10 days later once he had all the facts,
once he had sent a delegation down to look

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:03.570
at what had happened in Grenada, and I asked
some questions, he came back.

00:31:03.570 --> 00:31:04.960
He said, "Whoops, I was wrong.

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:06.920
It was indeed a good operation."

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:09.720
How do you deal with that kind of situation
if you're the president and you're trying

00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:12.000
to have some kind of reasonable rational decision-making
process?

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:17.130
Dick Cheney: Isn't Grenada a classic
example not of a decision to go to war but

00:31:17.130 --> 00:31:21.270
rather of a commander-in-chief to take action
to save American lives and, therefore the

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:24.670
War Powers Act probably shouldn't even apply
at all even if we buy your arguments about

00:31:24.670 --> 00:31:26.020
what the purpose of the Act is.

00:31:26.020 --> 00:31:29.200
Lee Hamilton: Well, I don't think so, Dick.

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:33.630
After all, we committed combat troops in a
very major way.

00:31:33.630 --> 00:31:35.700
Those combat troops met resistance.

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:37.370
They were fired upon.

00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:38.820
We sustained casualties.

00:31:38.820 --> 00:31:44.620
We had people killed in war there in the invasion
or in the rescue operation, whatever you wanna

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:45.620
say.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:50.960
Now, the fact that it turns out that the American
people strongly supported the action, and

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:55.640
I think the Congress strongly supported the
action, I don't think is the relevant point

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:57.290
here.

00:31:57.290 --> 00:32:01.320
There was a period of time when the president
was in the process of making the decision

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:04.380
should we go into Grenada.

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:09.290
The question of consultation, if you have
the right kind of a spirit prevailing here,

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:14.570
it seems to me it would be to invite congressional
leadership in.

00:32:14.570 --> 00:32:19.340
And no member of Congress could complain,
I think, if that leadership or the leadership

00:32:19.340 --> 00:32:26.200
of the two parties in both houses of the Congress
and restricted only to that group and say,

00:32:26.200 --> 00:32:28.580
"This is what I'm thinking about doing."

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:31.800
The important point is prior consultation.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:37.460
The order to invade or to go in for the rescue
had already been made.

00:32:37.460 --> 00:32:41.130
The president had signed that order prior
to the 8:00 meeting.

00:32:41.130 --> 00:32:43.670
He did not call those leaders in to consult.

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:49.740
He had already made up his mind to commit
American troops for combat purposes, and he

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:51.800
did not consult with the leaders of Congress.

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:55.340
Dick Cheney: If he had consulted, what might have
been the outcome?

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:57.040
Lee Hamilton: Well, I don't know.

00:32:57.040 --> 00:33:01.160
But you have to have...you have to give the
opportunity it seems to me.

00:33:01.160 --> 00:33:03.150
These are tough questions.

00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:07.500
Maybe the question is not as tough in the
Grenada situation as it would be in many,

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:08.520
many others.

00:33:08.520 --> 00:33:16.790
But the principal is it seems to me that the
most important decision that government makes

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:19.140
is whether or not to go to war.

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:22.650
That is the paramount decision that government
makes.

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:31.220
Should that decision be made by one person
or should it be made by that person, the president

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:33.240
and the Congress?

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:40.450
John Charles Daly: There were people who disagreed strongly with the executives exercise of War Powers

00:33:40.450 --> 00:33:47.560
Act but who did not want the War Powers Act
of 1973 on the grounds that that gave him

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:50.450
powers which were not his under the Constitution.

00:33:50.450 --> 00:33:53.180
That straight of debate ran continuously.

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:58.750
So the suggestion there is really that why
don't we stay where we are.

00:33:58.750 --> 00:34:03.480
Charles Mathias, Jr.: I don't think the War Powers Act alters
the president's constitutional powers.

00:34:03.480 --> 00:34:05.470
They are what they are.

00:34:05.470 --> 00:34:07.730
And the War Powers Act doesn't change that.

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:12.350
Now, that argument is made, I grant you.

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:17.590
It's applied most often I think to the initial
60-day period in which the president has an

00:34:17.590 --> 00:34:19.150
open license.

00:34:19.150 --> 00:34:26.130
They say that the Constitution doesn't give
him any days, 60, 30, 20, 10, any days and,

00:34:26.130 --> 00:34:32.060
therefore, to say that a president can act
during the 60-day period without congressional

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:35.370
authorization that somehow it enhances his
power.

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:37.390
I don't think it does that.

00:34:37.390 --> 00:34:41.850
That was a device that was built into the
law.

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:47.659
I think the War Powers Act doesn't prevent
the president from making foreign policy decisions.

00:34:47.659 --> 00:34:53.100
It simply says that they should be coordinated
with the Congress.

00:34:53.100 --> 00:34:57.250
Brent Scowcroft: I certainly agree that
there should be consultation and with the

00:34:57.250 --> 00:35:00.470
right spirit, it can be worked out.

00:35:00.470 --> 00:35:02.760
With the right spirit, you don't need the
War Powers Act.

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:07.200
With the wrong spirit, the War Powers Act
really doesn't affect the executive.

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:13.190
I mean, suppose the president consults as
broadly as you want and the verdict is that

00:35:13.190 --> 00:35:14.590
he shouldn't do something.

00:35:14.590 --> 00:35:17.580
There's nothing that prevents him from going
ahead and doing it.

00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:19.780
Man: That's right.

00:35:19.780 --> 00:35:20.910
Charles Mathias: For a while.

00:35:20.910 --> 00:35:21.910
Brent Scowcroft: For a while.

00:35:21.910 --> 00:35:22.910
John Charles Daly: But that was always there, wasn't it?

00:35:22.910 --> 00:35:23.910
In the power of...

00:35:23.910 --> 00:35:28.010
Brent Scowcroft: But then, for the while,
it seems to me you get into another clear

00:35:28.010 --> 00:35:30.400
issue of constitutionality.

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:38.030
And that is if the role of commander-in-chief
does not include the right to move troops

00:35:38.030 --> 00:35:41.910
around, then it is an absolute meaningless
term.

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:50.280
And the Congress irrigates it to itself by
its inaction to force the President to do

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:54.470
something which the Constitution says he doesn't
have to do.

00:35:54.470 --> 00:36:01.650
In other words, to pull the troops out by
its own inaction, not even by saying to the

00:36:01.650 --> 00:36:06.090
president he shouldn't do that for these or
that or the other reasons but just, because

00:36:06.090 --> 00:36:11.990
it's unable to make up its own line, it is
forcing the president not to behave as if

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:12.990
you're a commander-in- chief.

00:36:12.990 --> 00:36:16.170
Lee Hamilton: The point, Brent,
it seems to me is that the war-making powers

00:36:16.170 --> 00:36:18.610
are clearly divided in the Constitution.

00:36:18.610 --> 00:36:19.610
Brent Scowcroft: Clearly.

00:36:19.610 --> 00:36:20.980
Lee Hamilton: You don't give all
the power to the president.

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:22.910
You don't give all the power to the Congress.

00:36:22.910 --> 00:36:28.250
It's a shared power, and that's all we're
trying to say in the War Powers Act.

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:32.440
When you make a decision to go to war, let's
be sure it's a shared collective judgment.

00:36:32.440 --> 00:36:34.320
Brent Scowcroft: But Lee, it was shared
before.

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:41.490
Take the Cooper Church amendments in Vietnam
that cut off our use of military forces in

00:36:41.490 --> 00:36:44.050
and over Cambodia and so on and so forth.

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:49.840
There are many ways for the Congress to insert
in legislation the kinds of restriction that

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:51.660
feels are important.

00:36:51.660 --> 00:36:54.410
And without an item veto, the president is
stopped.

00:36:54.410 --> 00:36:59.270
John Charles Daly: Well, I think one thing, we've covered
this subject very, very broadly.

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:03.980
And I'm sure our audience would like to have
a chance to participate in, so I think it's

00:37:03.980 --> 00:37:05.680
time for the question and answer session.

00:37:05.680 --> 00:37:07.480
May I have the first question please?

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:11.430
John R: Yes, my name is John Rom [SP] with
the faculty at Clackamas Community College

00:37:11.430 --> 00:37:12.750
in Oregon City Oregon.

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:14.460
My question's for Representative Cheney.

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:19.930
In the event that the Supreme Court would
declare the War Powers Act of 1973 is unconstitutional

00:37:19.930 --> 00:37:24.150
due to infringing the president's power to
commit troops, what legislative alternatives

00:37:24.150 --> 00:37:25.880
would you propose in this case?

00:37:25.880 --> 00:37:31.720
Dick Cheney: My own personal view
is that we don't need the War Powers Act.

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:37.240
If the court strikes it down, I would expect
that it would be on the basis of the provision

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:43.120
that we discussed up here earlier that is
to say that it constitutes a legislative veto.

00:37:43.120 --> 00:37:46.890
The president doesn't have the opportunity
himself to veto that act by the Congress and

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:49.290
therefore it violates the separation of powers.

00:37:49.290 --> 00:37:56.130
I think as Mac, and Lee, and Brent have all
said that the hearts of having the relationship

00:37:56.130 --> 00:38:03.490
work between the president and the executive
branch and the Congress in this area is basically

00:38:03.490 --> 00:38:07.760
a requirement for good-faith effort on both
sides.

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:14.040
And my own personal view is that Congress
already has adequate restrictions and restraints

00:38:14.040 --> 00:38:18.250
and powers and authority on the book, on the
books so that they are in a position to be

00:38:18.250 --> 00:38:19.250
able to intervene.

00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:22.220
If a president does make a serious mistake,
they do have the power of the purse.

00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:28.390
They do have the opportunity to hold hearings
and conduct oversight investigations and decide

00:38:28.390 --> 00:38:31.980
whether or not Congress wants to sustain that
effort after the fact.

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:38.090
But that in the 1980s, in the years ahead,
we are very unlikely to find ourselves in

00:38:38.090 --> 00:38:44.240
a set of circumstances where the kind of conflict
that is envisioned in the War Powers Act is

00:38:44.240 --> 00:38:49.890
likely to arise and that presidents have to
be trusted to some extent that once they're

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:53.610
elected, we delegate to them enormous authority.

00:38:53.610 --> 00:38:58.930
Directly and indirectly, we build nuclear
weapon systems and we give them total control

00:38:58.930 --> 00:39:02.530
over those system without consultation with
the United States Congress whatsoever.

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:07.330
And it seems to me, having made that leap
of faith that we are, in fact, gonna trust

00:39:07.330 --> 00:39:09.470
the President to be commander in chief.

00:39:09.470 --> 00:39:13.370
Under those circumstances, we can also trust
him in other areas and that there's no need

00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:17.200
for a replacement, if you will, for the War
Powers Act should that be struck down.

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:18.200
John Charles Daly: Senator Mathias.

00:39:18.200 --> 00:39:23.900
Charles Mathias, Jr.: I think there is a subjective
value to the War Powers Act that perhaps hasn't

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:26.580
been sufficiently appreciated.

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:32.270
During the debate in which it was first adopted,
Senator Javits, used to say that it was like

00:39:32.270 --> 00:39:38.750
a sign at a railroad crossing, that it was
simply saying stop, look, and listen before

00:39:38.750 --> 00:39:41.700
you embark on this journey overseas.

00:39:41.700 --> 00:39:46.200
And I think that that's the effect that we
hoped it would have on the executive branch.

00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:52.050
But there is a subjective effect on the legislative
branch, which is not without some importance.

00:39:52.050 --> 00:40:00.110
And that is that in a non-adversarial way,
it provides a framework in which you can examine

00:40:00.110 --> 00:40:02.820
an operation.

00:40:02.820 --> 00:40:10.210
When I introduced the resolution, I think
it was in 1969, to repeal the Gulf of Tonkin

00:40:10.210 --> 00:40:14.570
Resolution, finally took up Lyndon Johnson's
challenge.

00:40:14.570 --> 00:40:20.390
That was, in fact, an adversarial action.

00:40:20.390 --> 00:40:26.830
But the kind of periodic reviews that are
provided in the War Powers Act don't have

00:40:26.830 --> 00:40:29.340
to be adversarial.

00:40:29.340 --> 00:40:33.910
People will take sides within the debate,
but the existence of the debate itself becomes

00:40:33.910 --> 00:40:40.040
a matter of procedure and is neutral as to
the event.

00:40:40.040 --> 00:40:46.320
Charles Mathias, Jr.: I would like to suggest that
that is not the way it is viewed by the executive

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:52.440
branch, that it is an irritant to the executive
branch, and considered broadly a congressional

00:40:52.440 --> 00:40:55.870
attempt to infringe on the prerogatives of
the President.

00:40:55.870 --> 00:41:02.710
And in fact, it prevents the kind of freewill
cooperative consultation, which I think is

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:06.040
the fundamentally real answer to our problem.

00:41:06.040 --> 00:41:10.620
Dick Cheney: I come back to the basic notion, Mac,
that I don't think we can find an example

00:41:10.620 --> 00:41:16.751
in modern times when a president hasn't done
what the War Powers Act expects him to do

00:41:16.751 --> 00:41:20.510
anyway to the extent that we talk about consultation
and notification.

00:41:20.510 --> 00:41:25.320
I don't know of anybody, who served in the
presidency in the last 30 or 40 or 50 years

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:31.140
and probably longer than that if I went back
far enough, who would have made a decision

00:41:31.140 --> 00:41:38.890
in n high-handed arrogant fashion to commit
troops in a way that ultimately lacked the

00:41:38.890 --> 00:41:42.140
kind of public support, broad public support
that it requires.

00:41:42.140 --> 00:41:45.550
Certainly, that wasn't Lyndon Johnson's intent
when he got involved in Southeast Asia.

00:41:45.550 --> 00:41:48.430
Certainly, that wasn't the congressional intent
when they supported it.

00:41:48.430 --> 00:41:51.600
In the end, public opinion swung against it.

00:41:51.600 --> 00:41:58.420
But it took years of developments and gradual
constant escalation of our involvement therein,

00:41:58.420 --> 00:42:03.080
but there's nothing in the War Powers Act
had it been in place in 1964 or '65 that it

00:42:03.080 --> 00:42:04.080
would change that.

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:08.860
I think we do have to rely to some extent
on good faith interpretation of our responsibilities

00:42:08.860 --> 00:42:11.330
both as legislators and executives.

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:16.600
And I think that history would bear out the
statement that in fact that good-faith effort

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:18.080
has been there in virtually every case.

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:19.080
John Charles Daly: Senator.

00:42:19.080 --> 00:42:23.580
Charles Mathias, Jr.: One of the premises of which
we are meeting is to discuss constitutionality

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:27.330
and therefore I dragged you back to the history
of the Constitution which...

00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:28.330
John Charles Daly: Thank you.

00:42:28.330 --> 00:42:29.330
Senator.

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:33.550
Charles Mathias, Jr.: ...which I think is pertinent.

00:42:33.550 --> 00:42:43.380
The executive side of this argument is presented
as the need to reposed confidence in the judgment

00:42:43.380 --> 00:42:47.180
of the president and his advisors.

00:42:47.180 --> 00:42:52.860
Alexander Hamilton was never thought to be
soft on the executive.

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:57.290
He was always viewed as a strong executive
advocate.

00:42:57.290 --> 00:43:03.070
What he said in writing, the "Federalist Papers"
that the history of human conduct does not

00:43:03.070 --> 00:43:10.740
warrant that exalted opinion of human virtue,
which would make it wise in a nation to commit

00:43:10.740 --> 00:43:18.240
interests of so delicate and momentous a kind
as those which concern intercourse with the

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:26.480
rest of the world to the sole disposal of
a magistrate created in circumstance as would

00:43:26.480 --> 00:43:29.630
be a President of the United States.

00:43:29.630 --> 00:43:41.310
And I think there is a kind of concern expressed
at the very birth of the Republic that reflects

00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:44.960
the problems that we are discussing here this
evening.

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:46.471
Brent Scowcroft: I would agree with that.

00:43:46.471 --> 00:43:52.480
But I think, you know, to imagine that the
world is different and therefore the framers,

00:43:52.480 --> 00:43:57.620
in a sense, didn't know what they were doing
or what have you is wrong.

00:43:57.620 --> 00:44:07.860
There's been a recent study that about 10%
of the military engagements between 1780 and

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:13.200
1880 were consequent to a declaration of war
that is around the world.

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:18.240
So that the idea that undeclared wars are
a recent phenomenon is certainly not true.

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:23.890
Charles Mathias, Jr.: But the degree is different but the circumstances
are pretty standard.

00:44:23.890 --> 00:44:30.520
Brent Scowcroft: In the process of writing the Constitution
as I think...I can't remember who said it

00:44:30.520 --> 00:44:36.170
now, the words to make war were deliberately
changed to declare war.

00:44:36.170 --> 00:44:37.930
Now, that cannot have been accidental.

00:44:37.930 --> 00:44:39.010
John Charles Daly: It wasn't accidental.

00:44:39.010 --> 00:44:41.070
It was a result of debate.

00:44:41.070 --> 00:44:46.190
Brent Scowcroft: And it seems to me that that's a very
important change.

00:44:46.190 --> 00:44:50.390
One is in a sense recognizing a situation
if you will.

00:44:50.390 --> 00:44:51.470
The other is initiating.

00:44:51.470 --> 00:45:00.560
Charles Mathias, Jr.: But Hamilton who was there argues that
the power there was not vested solely in the

00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:01.570
President and should not have not been.

00:45:01.570 --> 00:45:02.570
Brent Scowcroft: And it is not.

00:45:02.570 --> 00:45:03.570
Charles Mathias: And should not have been.

00:45:03.570 --> 00:45:09.970
Brent Scowcroft: And it is not because the Congress raises
and maintains armies.

00:45:09.970 --> 00:45:13.940
John Charles Daly: Well, we have to get another question.

00:45:13.940 --> 00:45:16.090
There's somebody...You're next, are you sir?

00:45:16.090 --> 00:45:17.090
Yes, sir.

00:45:17.090 --> 00:45:22.800
Robert: I'm Robert Goldwyn, director of constitutional
studies of American Enterprise Institute.

00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:28.040
Senator Mathias, that passage from Alexander
Hamilton you just quoted was in reference,

00:45:28.040 --> 00:45:35.410
not to making war or declaring war, but in
reference to the power of the constitutional

00:45:35.410 --> 00:45:37.900
advice and consent on treaties.

00:45:37.900 --> 00:45:45.390
And I think that raises an interesting question
related to what Congressman Hamilton was saying

00:45:45.390 --> 00:45:52.600
about bringing Congress into the decision-making.

00:45:52.600 --> 00:45:59.180
Do you mean some kind of consenting role in
the same sense as advise and consent with

00:45:59.180 --> 00:46:07.381
regard to treaties, that is would you really
try to restrict the power of the President

00:46:07.381 --> 00:46:16.910
to the extent that, if when he did consult
with members of Congress and they gave him

00:46:16.910 --> 00:46:24.850
opposing advice or conflicting advice as congressmen
and senators want to do, that is they disagree

00:46:24.850 --> 00:46:27.680
among themselves, then what does the president
do?

00:46:27.680 --> 00:46:33.100
Do you really mean that the congressman should
have some role in the decision-making power,

00:46:33.100 --> 00:46:41.120
because that then puts in real question the
whole principle of separation of powers?

00:46:41.120 --> 00:46:48.911
Charles Mathias, Jr.: I think we made that clear
in reference to General Scowcroft's question

00:46:48.911 --> 00:46:54.300
about whether members of the legislative branch
should serve on the National Security Council.

00:46:54.300 --> 00:46:57.820
I think that clearly would be a violation
of separation of powers.

00:46:57.820 --> 00:47:01.770
And under the American Constitution as we
know it today, it wouldn't be proper.

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:02.901
And I'm not suggesting that.

00:47:02.901 --> 00:47:08.270
I think what Hamilton was looking at was foreign
policy, and war is, of course, the ultimate

00:47:08.270 --> 00:47:13.000
extension of foreign policy, the ultimate
act of foreign policy.

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:21.000
And so, I think it's clearly comprehended
within his thinking.

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:33.140
It seems to me that what we're saying is that
there must be a coordinate decision made now.

00:47:33.140 --> 00:47:41.280
I don't see how we can ever embody in a statute
precisely how this will occur.

00:47:41.280 --> 00:47:44.760
Lee Hamilton has one kind of personality.

00:47:44.760 --> 00:47:48.420
Dick Cheney has a kind of personality.

00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:52.960
There will be presidents with which one of
them will be simpatico and presidents which

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:54.970
the other one will not.

00:47:54.970 --> 00:47:59.530
And these human factors get into the act.

00:47:59.530 --> 00:48:05.100
General Scowcroft and Dick Cheney both having
served in the highest positions in the White

00:48:05.100 --> 00:48:14.700
House know how personalities have become important
elements of policymaking and decision execution.

00:48:14.700 --> 00:48:21.590
But I think that's one reason that it's impossible
to create an absolutely rigid hidebound system

00:48:21.590 --> 00:48:26.690
of consultation, that from administration
to administration, it's gonna change a little

00:48:26.690 --> 00:48:27.690
bit.

00:48:27.690 --> 00:48:36.370
But I think the factor is that the president
be exposed to all the considerations before

00:48:36.370 --> 00:48:38.900
the decision is made.

00:48:38.900 --> 00:48:44.720
Lee Hamilton: You ask should the Congress consent.

00:48:44.720 --> 00:48:47.430
Why shouldn't the Congress consent?

00:48:47.430 --> 00:48:54.350
What's wrong in a democracy by making a judgment
in a democratic manner?

00:48:54.350 --> 00:48:55.350
That's all we're asking.

00:48:55.350 --> 00:49:00.740
Brent Scowcroft: Are you saying that consent should be
mandatory on the president, should be binding

00:49:00.740 --> 00:49:01.740
on the president?

00:49:01.740 --> 00:49:07.380
Lee Hamilton: No, the question was, should the Congress
consent to the decision.

00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:10.900
And I see nothing wrong with the Congress
consenting.

00:49:10.900 --> 00:49:17.491
Dick Cheney: Again, Lee, we come back to the proposition
that most of the times when military force

00:49:17.491 --> 00:49:23.060
has been used in recent years simply haven't
been circumstances which let themselves to

00:49:23.060 --> 00:49:24.060
that kind of process.

00:49:24.060 --> 00:49:27.141
Lee Hamilton: What kind of a war can the president
of the United States carry out if the Congress

00:49:27.141 --> 00:49:29.350
of the United States doesn't consent?

00:49:29.350 --> 00:49:34.290
Dick Cheney: But in many respects, again, we've already
given prior approval in a sense.

00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:39.520
We have appropriated the funds, and raised
the army, and purchased the equipment, and

00:49:39.520 --> 00:49:45.020
built the missiles and the bombers, and said,
"Okay, here now you have the authority to

00:49:45.020 --> 00:49:48.840
make decisions about how we're gonna use certain
elements of that."

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:54.619
That suggests that you can go to the Congress
and involve the Congress as deeply as I think

00:49:54.619 --> 00:49:59.390
you would like to in the decision in advance
of the actual commitment of troops just strikes

00:49:59.390 --> 00:50:01.750
me is not very practical.

00:50:01.750 --> 00:50:09.930
Charles Mathais, Jr.: I simply have to raise the caveat that
the existence of the defense establishment

00:50:09.930 --> 00:50:16.520
is, in some way, an authorization to the President
to start out on foreign adventures.

00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:17.690
I don't believe that.

00:50:17.690 --> 00:50:20.410
Dick Cheney: It's a recognition that we live in a
dangerous and hostile world.

00:50:20.410 --> 00:50:24.520
And from time to time, we may have to defend
ourselves and defend of our interest and that

00:50:24.520 --> 00:50:29.180
the individual we look to make those decisions,
especially those that take on the nature of

00:50:29.180 --> 00:50:33.890
a decision of a commander-in-chief rather
than a legislative body, we entrust to the

00:50:33.890 --> 00:50:34.890
president.

00:50:34.890 --> 00:50:39.600
Charles Mathias, Jr.: But I think, as Brent Scowcroft had said,
we entrust that final decision to the president.

00:50:39.600 --> 00:50:45.380
But we have confidence that Secretary of State
will have his say, that the Secretary of Defense

00:50:45.380 --> 00:50:52.110
will have some comment, that the Joint Chiefs
of Staff will talk about the military feasibility,

00:50:52.110 --> 00:50:57.980
that the Central Intelligence Agency will
provide basic information, that the whole

00:50:57.980 --> 00:51:00.600
operatives of government will be brought to
bear.

00:51:00.600 --> 00:51:06.710
Now, at some point in that decision-making
process, and I certainly would not have meant

00:51:06.710 --> 00:51:13.630
to imply, that I thought that this is something
the president decides in the shower bath on

00:51:13.630 --> 00:51:22.490
his own, that somewhere in this process, there
is room and time to register the congressional

00:51:22.490 --> 00:51:23.490
point of view.

00:51:23.490 --> 00:51:26.830
Lee Hamilton: I think you have to draw a distinction
between two situations.

00:51:26.830 --> 00:51:28.330
One is the emergency situation.

00:51:28.330 --> 00:51:30.660
Dick, you refer to that awful frequently.

00:51:30.660 --> 00:51:34.740
And I think you're probably correct in that.

00:51:34.740 --> 00:51:37.730
There are gonna be times when a president
has to act.

00:51:37.730 --> 00:51:39.670
Arguably, that was Grenada.

00:51:39.670 --> 00:51:43.210
The American citizens had to be rescued right
now.

00:51:43.210 --> 00:51:45.050
And the president really didn't have time.

00:51:45.050 --> 00:51:50.690
I can see how you can make a case for the
president moving very quickly on that.

00:51:50.690 --> 00:51:55.480
But that's not the only time a president is
or the only manner in which a president is

00:51:55.480 --> 00:51:59.010
going to be confronted with the decision to
commit troops.

00:51:59.010 --> 00:52:02.300
Should we invade Nicaragua today?

00:52:02.300 --> 00:52:07.290
That's a possible question on the agenda of
the country.

00:52:07.290 --> 00:52:12.490
If I were a president reflecting on that question
today, I'd like to call Mac Mathias and talk

00:52:12.490 --> 00:52:16.910
with him about it, and talk with some others
about it too.

00:52:16.910 --> 00:52:17.990
And that's what we're saying.

00:52:17.990 --> 00:52:23.130
And it seems to me that kind of a situation
is very different from the emergency situation.

00:52:23.130 --> 00:52:25.810
Brent Scowcroft: I think we need to be clear about another
thing.

00:52:25.810 --> 00:52:29.990
And that is War Powers Act does not say the
president will not attack this or that or

00:52:29.990 --> 00:52:30.990
the other.

00:52:30.990 --> 00:52:35.950
It really says the president may not move
troops.

00:52:35.950 --> 00:52:39.560
That's what it's talking about, may not introduce
troops in certain areas.

00:52:39.560 --> 00:52:41.390
It seems to me that is fundamental.

00:52:41.390 --> 00:52:45.020
Charles Mathias, Jr.: In certain circumstances in which hostilities
are imminent.

00:52:45.020 --> 00:52:46.020
Brent Scowcroft: That's right.

00:52:46.020 --> 00:52:51.650
But it's back to Dick's point about having
raised armies, what can the president do with

00:52:51.650 --> 00:52:52.650
them?

00:52:52.650 --> 00:52:58.030
And it seems to me that if the commander-in-chief
cannot deploy and move his forces around,

00:52:58.030 --> 00:53:01.240
then the term commander-in-chief is absolutely
meaningless.

00:53:01.240 --> 00:53:05.260
We're not talking about specifically going
to war here.

00:53:05.260 --> 00:53:08.810
There is nothing about that in the War Powers
Act.

00:53:08.810 --> 00:53:12.110
Charles Mathias, Jr.: We're talking about hostilities, and
that is in the War Powers Act.

00:53:12.110 --> 00:53:14.060
Brent Scowcroft: Where it is possible.

00:53:14.060 --> 00:53:20.800
We're not talking about the president using
forces offensively deliberately to attack.

00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:22.080
John Charles Daly: All right.

00:53:22.080 --> 00:53:23.369
Next question please.

00:53:23.369 --> 00:53:24.400
Who has the microphone?

00:53:24.400 --> 00:53:25.400
All right.

00:53:25.400 --> 00:53:26.400
We get to microphone.

00:53:26.400 --> 00:53:31.820
Terry: I'm Terry Emerson, counsel to Senator
Barry Goldwater who voted against the War

00:53:31.820 --> 00:53:33.240
Powers Resolution.

00:53:33.240 --> 00:53:39.230
I make a quick comment on Alexander Hamilton
because I've read his works in a famous set

00:53:39.230 --> 00:53:42.670
of written debates with James Madison.

00:53:42.670 --> 00:53:47.580
Hamilton did make a clear distinction between
offensive war and defensive war.

00:53:47.580 --> 00:53:53.320
He said the Congress can commence offensive
war through the declaration power, but the

00:53:53.320 --> 00:53:58.350
president possesses the defensive power to
initiate defensive actions on behalf of the

00:53:58.350 --> 00:53:59.680
United States.

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:07.930
My question to the panel would be to any member
who would wish to make a test of the War Powers

00:54:07.930 --> 00:54:13.080
Resolution against the history immediately
preceding World War II.

00:54:13.080 --> 00:54:17.500
Take the actions of President Franklin Roosevelt
in 1941.

00:54:17.500 --> 00:54:24.020
In 1940, Congress had barely renewed the military
draft by a single vote.

00:54:24.020 --> 00:54:31.770
Yet in 1941, President Roosevelt, before any
declaration of war, sent American Marines

00:54:31.770 --> 00:54:35.910
equipped for combat to both Greenland and
Iceland.

00:54:35.910 --> 00:54:40.990
He gave them orders to cooperate fully with
British armed forces who were already there

00:54:40.990 --> 00:54:44.700
defending the islands against Nazi Germany.

00:54:44.700 --> 00:54:51.340
Franklin Roosevelt also ordered the American
Navy to escort British shipping in the North

00:54:51.340 --> 00:54:57.720
Atlantic and to shoot on sight against German
U-boats, all this before any declaration of

00:54:57.720 --> 00:54:58.770
war.

00:54:58.770 --> 00:55:04.350
Under the War Powers Resolution, Congress
would have been faced with having to vote

00:55:04.350 --> 00:55:08.710
within 60 days on whether to extend President
Roosevelt's powers.

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:15.630
I asked whether testing the War Powers Resolution
in this situation would not have led to a

00:55:15.630 --> 00:55:22.420
total disaster for the democracies of the
world through an isolationist Congress refusing

00:55:22.420 --> 00:55:26.540
to ratify President Roosevelt's wise actions.

00:55:26.540 --> 00:55:29.270
John Charles Daly: Who would like to start?

00:55:29.270 --> 00:55:34.460
Dick Cheney: I'll simply say that the gentlemen I
think stated the case eloquently and he's

00:55:34.460 --> 00:55:35.710
absolutely right.

00:55:35.710 --> 00:55:37.310
John Charles Daly: Sir.

00:55:37.310 --> 00:55:47.380
Charles Mathias, Jr.: Well, I think it's only a hypothetical
judgment is what might have happened if the

00:55:47.380 --> 00:55:50.350
War Powers Act had been in effect.

00:55:50.350 --> 00:55:53.740
It is so difficult to project.

00:55:53.740 --> 00:55:59.680
It it's true just by way of example how unpredictable
history is.

00:55:59.680 --> 00:56:04.770
It's true that Senator Goldwater voted against
the War Powers Resolution, but thereafter,

00:56:04.770 --> 00:56:11.570
he called for the immediate withdrawal of
the Marines from Lebanon.

00:56:11.570 --> 00:56:14.470
I don't know what real conclusions you can
draw.

00:56:14.470 --> 00:56:19.480
John Charles Daly: This concludes another public policy
